        "The title uses $10 million as the target, but a plan is meaningless unless the metric is defined.\nMetric\tMeaning\tWhy it matters\nGross sales / revenue\tValue of products sold before relevant deductions\tShows commercial scale\nNet revenue\tRevenue after discounts, refunds, and other applicable adjustments\tBetter view of realized sales\nGross profit\tRevenue minus cost of goods sold\tShows product-level economics\nContribution profit\tRevenue less variable costs such as product, fulfilment, payment, and acquisition costs\tShows whether growth adds economic value\nOperating profit\tProfit after operating expenses\tShows business profitability\nCash flow\tActual cash entering and leaving the business\tDetermines whether bills and inventory can be paid\nValuation\tEstimated value of the business in a transaction or financing context\tNot the same as cash earned",
        "A business can report $10 million in sales and still run out of cash. It can be profitable on paper while cash is trapped in inventory, receivables, deposits, or supplier lead times.\nSet a target hierarchy\nFor this playbook, use the following planning hierarchy:\n1. Prove that a specific customer wants the product.\n2. Prove that each order can generate positive contribution after variable costs.\n3. Prove that the acquisition channel can produce customers at an acceptable cost.\n4. Prove that suppliers can replenish the product reliably.\n5. Prove that customer experience remains strong as order volume rises.\n6. Scale revenue only while the first five conditions remain healthy.\nThe $10 million target is an ambition. The operating system is what makes growth sustainable.\nPart II — Find Demand Before You Build the Business"
