        "The founder launched more than ten brands and eventually spent very little time on the original weighted-blanket business, despite it generating substantial revenue.\nWhy this failed\nThe businesses and their financials were merged into one account. It became difficult to understand:\n- Which brand was profitable.\n- Which team was responsible for which result.\n- Where money was being spent.\n- Which business needed attention.\n- Whether the overall group was actually healthy.\nThe organization also grew to approximately 100 people. Communication became complex, output was difficult to track, and motivation became harder.\nThe deeper problem: no clear accountability\nThe founder says employees were not properly incentivized around the success or failure of the original business.",
        "When nobody clearly owns a result, the founder may assume that the organization is handling it while the team assumes another person is responsible.\nThe practical correction\nFor every brand or business unit, define:\n- One accountable leader.\n- A separate profit-and-loss view.\n- A clear budget.\n- Specific targets.\n- A weekly reporting cadence.\n- Decision rights.\n- Incentives connected to outcomes the person can influence.\nDo not launch a second business to soothe anxiety\nA second business is not automatically diversification. It can become a distraction that consumes the resources needed to protect the first business.",
