        "A product flywheel is a repeatable system in which each successful product strengthens the next launch.\nA practical product flywheel\n1. Understand a specific customer segment.\n2. Launch a differentiated product for that segment.\n3. Acquire customers profitably.\n4. Learn from sales, reviews, returns, and support.\n5. Use those insights to design the next product.\n6. Offer the next product to an existing audience.\n7. Improve retention, average order value, or referral.\n8. Repeat without weakening the brand.\nThe source does not provide a fully specified flywheel formula. This sequence is an operational interpretation of the founder's reflection.\nWhat makes a product range defensible?\nPotential sources of differentiation include:\n- Distinctive design.\n- Proprietary or exclusive features.\n- Licensed or original artwork with proper rights.\n- Better materials or construction.\n- Strong customer experience.\n- Trusted brand positioning.\n- Product combinations that solve a broader need.\n- Retail or distribution relationships.\nDo not assume that adding more SKUs creates defensibility. More products can simply create more inventory risk."
